We know that two points form an edge if they lie on opposite sides of the diagonal.  Since these points have different $x$-coordinates but same $y$-coordinate, one must be above (or below) the other along the x-axis. Thus both point coordinates need to satisfy: \[
\begin{aligned}
(2,1) &\rightarrow \text{(horizontal)}\\
(3,4) &\rightarrow \text{(vertical)}
\end{aligned}.\]The distance between them forms the side length of the square, so we compute this using the difference formula for squares. The vertical distance is \( |4 - 1| = 3 \). The horizontal distance from \( (2,1) \) to \( (3,4) \) is \( |3 - 2| = 1 \), which means the side lengths are equal. So it's a perfect square with side length √2. The area is then \( (\sqrt{2})^2 = \boxed{2} \).
